  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Mostly pleasant conditions on tap today until \nscattered showers move in later this afternoon and linger \nthrough the night.\n\nHigh pressure sitting over Maine will push into the Canadian \nMaritimes today, lending clear skies and cooler temperatures in \nthe mid to low 60s for most of the day. A weak low then pushes \neast off the Delmarva coast, bringing chances for showers to \nsouthern New England into the afternoon and overnight hours. \nMost of the latest guidance suite is indicating this will be a \nscattered event, with the heaviest rain bands staying offshore; \nhowever, the NAMNST and HREF are the most aggressive, not only \nindicating more of a stratiform rain, but in bringing some \nheavier bands inland. Highest confidence in heaviest rainfall \nremains along the south coast, Cape, and Islands, especially \nafter 7PM and through the overnight hours.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A sharp temperature drop is then expected for \nthe start of the week followed by a return to dry, fall-like \nconditions.\n\nA deepening low is expected to track east across the James Bay \nand into Quebec Monday with a strong high pressures system over \nthe Great Lakes following on its heels for the start of the work\nweek. A significantly cooler airmass sinks south into New \nEngland, with the latest guidance indicating the 540mb line \nsinking well into northern New England, possibly as far south as\nsouthern NH, by early Tuesday morning. 850mb temperatures \nlikely drop to near or just below 0C by Tuesday morning. Most \nguidance also suggests 925mb temps drop to as low at +1C or +2C.\nThis would support surface highs near 60 degrees with nighttime\nlows possibly falling into the 30s through Wednesday.\n\nA brief warm-up to the low 70s may be possible late week as the\nhigh pushes east Thursday. Otherwise, prolonged west-southwest \nflow will mark the return of drier conditions and seasonable \ntemperatures to southern New England for the end of the week.",
